1. What is Ansible Galaxy?

Ansible Galaxy is a community platform where Ansible users can share and reuse automation code.

It provides mainly:

a) Ansible Roles
b) Ansible Collections

Instead of writing every Ansible task from the beginning, we can find an existing role from Ansible Galaxy and use it in our playbook.

For example, suppose we need to install Docker on a server.

We may need to:

=> Install Docker packages
=> Configure Docker
=> Start Docker service
=> Enable Docker service
=> Handle different Linux distributions

Instead of writing all these tasks ourselves, we can search Ansible Galaxy for a Docker role and reuse it.

2. Ansible Galaxy vs Docker Hub

Ansible Galaxy can be compared with Docker Hub.

a) Docker Hub provides ready-made Docker images:

Docker Hub
    |
    +-- nginx image
    +-- ubuntu image
    +-- mysql image

We can download an image using:

docker pull nginx

Similarly, Ansible Galaxy provides reusable Ansible roles:

b) Ansible Galaxy
    |
    +-- Docker role
    +-- Nginx role
    +-- Apache role
    +-- MySQL role

We can download a role using:

ansible-galaxy role install <namespace>.<role>

The command-line utility used to work with Ansible Galaxy is:

ansible-galaxy

3. Why use Ansible Galaxy?

Ansible Galaxy can save time because we don't always need to write common automation tasks ourselves.

Benefits:

=> Reuse existing automation
=> Save development time
=> Keep playbooks smaller
=> Make automation modular
=> Learn from other people's Ansible roles
=> Share our own roles with others

However, we should not blindly use any role in production.

Before using a third-party role, check:

a) Author
b) 
c) GitHub repository
d) Supported operating systems
e) Version
f) Dependencies
g) Maintenance/activity
h) Role source code

4. Ansible Galaxy Command

Check the available options:

ansible-galaxy --help

We can also check the installed version:

ansible-galaxy --version

Ansible Galaxy supports different types of content. For this lab, we are mainly focusing on Roles.

5. What is an Ansible Role?

An Ansible Role is a way to organize Ansible automation into a standard directory structure.

A role can contain:

tasks/
handlers/
defaults/
vars/
templates/
files/
meta/

For example:

docker-role/
├── tasks/
├── handlers/
├── defaults/
├── vars/
├── templates/
├── files/
└── meta/

The role contains the actual Ansible automation.

Our playbook can then simply use the role.

Example:

roles:
  - bsmeding.docker
